<section xml:id="sect-multiplication-division">
  <title>Multiplication and Division</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The other two standard arithmetic operations are multiplication and division.
      These are tied together much like addition and subtraction are.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Multiplication</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Multiplication is defined as repeated addition.
        The result of <m>a \times b</m> is the second number <m>b</m> added to itself <m>a</m> times.
        For example
        <me>
          4 \times 6 = \underbrace{6+6+6+6}_{\text{4 times}}
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        In multiplication, the numbers are called the <alert>factors</alert> and the result is the <alert>product</alert>.
        For example,
        <me>
          \underbrace{4}_{\text{factor}}\times \underbrace{6}_{\text{factor}} = \underbrace{24}_{\text{product}}
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The notation for multiplication in early elementary school is <m>\times</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              In higher levels, a dot is used instead, for example <m>4\cdot6</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Set Model</title>

        <p>
          Use the Set model, the product $4 \times 6$ is interpreted as 4 groups of 6 objects.
          For example, if you have 4 bags each containing 6 oranges.
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                \foreach \y in {0,1.5,3} {
                  \foreach \x in {0,1.5,...,10.5} \draw (\x,\y) pic[scale=0.5] {orange}; 
                  }
                  \foreach \i in {0,1,2,3} 
                    \draw[dashed] ({-0.6+\i*3},-0.6) rectangle ({2.1+\i*3},3.7);
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </paragraphs>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Measurement Model</title>

        <p>
          In this model, $4 \times 6$ is interpreted as 4 hops on the number line each hop of length 6.
          An example, is that if you have 4 pieces of string, each piece is 6 inches.
          How long in total is the string?
        </p>

        <image width="80%">
          <shortdescription>
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.4]
                \draw[->] (-1,0) -- (25,0); 
                \foreach \x in {0,1,...,24} {\draw (\x,-0.2) -- (\x,0.2);} 
                \foreach \x in {0,6,12,...,24} {\draw (\x,-0.2) node [below] {$\x$};} \foreach \x in {0,6,12,...,18} {
                  \draw[->] (\x,0) .. controls ({\x+2},1) and ({\x+4},1) ..
                      ({\x+6},0);}
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </paragraphs>


      <paragraphs>
        <title>Array Model</title>

        <p>
          Another model to show multiplication is using a rectangular grid or rectangular array.
          The example $4 \times 6$ is illustrated in each of the following ways:
        </p>

        <sidebyside>
          <image width="75%">
            <shortdescription>
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
                  \foreach \x in {1,...,6} { \foreach \y in {1,...,4} { \fill (\x,\y) circle (3pt);}}
                \end{tikzpicture} 
                ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>

          <image width="75%">
            <shortdescription>
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
                \draw (0,0) grid (6,4);
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
          Note: be careful of using the interpretation of multiplication as area until the appropriate age.
          Multiplication is learned quite early, whereas the notation of area is delayed a year or two.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </introduction>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Properties of Multiplication</title>

      <axiom xml:id="prop-multiplication">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following are the axiomatic properties of multiplication, which holds for all whole numbers <m>a,b</m> and <m>c</m>:
          </p>

          <p>
            <dl>
            <li>
              <title>Multiplicative Identity</title>

              <p>
                <m>1 \times a = a</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <title>Commutative Property:</title>

              <p>
                <m>a \times b = b \times a</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <title>Associative Property</title>

              <p>
                <m>a \times (b \times c) = (a \times b) \times c</m>
              </p>
            </li>
            </dl>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </axiom>

      <p>
        Let's look at these properties more in depth.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Recalling that multiplication is repeated addition, <m>1 \times a</m> means adding <m>a</m> one time, so the answer is <m>a</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              This makes sense since you can regroup objects.
              4 groups of 5 oranges is the same as 5 groups of 4 oranges.
              A nice way to show this is with a set model.
              The following shows 4 groups of 5 as:
            </p>

            <image width="80%">
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach \y in {0,1.5,3,4.5} \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5,6} \draw (\x,\y) pic [scale=0.5] {orange}; 
                  \foreach \i in {0,1,2,3} \draw[dashed] (-0.5,{-0.6+\i*1.5}) rectangle (6.7,{0.6+\i*1.5});
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              and the following shows 5 groups of 4.
            </p>

            <image width="80%">
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach \y in {0,1.5,3,4.5} \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5,6} \draw (\x,\y) pic [scale=0.5]{orange}; 
                  \foreach \i in {0,1,2,3,4} \draw[dashed] ({-0.6+\i*1.5},-0.6) rectangle ({0.6+\i*1.5},5.2);
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              Notice in this that the oranges (objects) were not rearranged in anyway, but instead the grouping was done.
              This is important.
            </p>

            <p>
              Using the Rectangular Array model, you can interchange the rows and columns to explain the commutative identity, since the total number of boxes (dots) stay the same.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The Associative property adds another level of groups.
              The operation <m>3 \times (4 \times 5)</m> can be thought of 3 boxes each containing 4 bags each with 5 apples.
              The following diagram shows this.
            </p>

            <image width="80%">
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
                  \foreach \k in {0,1,2} {
                    \foreach \y in {0,1.5,3,4.5,6.0} \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5} \draw ({\x+7*\k},\y) pic[scale=0.5] {apple}; 
                    \foreach \i in {0,1,2,3} \draw[dashed] ({-0.6+\i*1.5+\k*7},-0.6) rectangle ({0.6+\i*1.5+\k*7},6.7);
                    \draw[thick, rounded corners] ({-0.8+\k*7},-0.8) rectangle ({5.5+\k*7},7);
                  }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              This is identical to <m>(3 \times 4) \times 5</m> or 5 boxes, each containing 3 bags of 4 apples which can be showed with
            </p>

            <image width="80%">
              <shortdescription>
              </shortdescription>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
                  \foreach \y in {0,2,...,8} {
                    \foreach \k in {0,1,2} {
                      \foreach \x in {0,1.5,3,4.5} \draw ({\x+7*\k},\y) pic[scale=0.5] {apple}; 
                    \draw[dashed] ({7*\k-0.6},{\y-0.6}) rectangle ({7*\k+5},{\y+0.7});
                    }
                    \draw[thick, rounded corners] (-0.8,{\y-0.8}) rectangle(19.5,{\y+0.9}); }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>

            <p>
              This shows the result 5 groups of 3 groups of 4 or
              <me>
                3 \times (4 \times 5) = (3 \times 4) \times 5
              </me>
              which shows the associative property.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            What property is each of the following?
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>2 \times (6 \times 4) = (2 \times 6) \times 4</m>. This is the multiplicative associative property.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>1 \times 6 = 6</m>. This is the multiplicative identity.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>3 \times 7 = 7 \times 3</m>. This is the multiplicative associative property.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <lemma>
        <title>Any-Order property of Multiplication</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            The operation <m>a \times b \times c</m> can be done in any order.
            In fact, the product of any number of factors can be done in any order.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </lemma>

      <axiom xml:id="prop-distributive-multiplication">
        <title>Distributive Property of Multiplication</title>

        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>a, b</m> and <m>c</m> are whole numbers then
            <me>
              a \times (b + c) = a \times b + a \times c
            </me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </axiom>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Thinking Strategies for Multiplication</title>

      <p>
        As with the previous operations, there are some important ways to think about multiplication.
        There are a few concepts.
      </p>

      <p>
        <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Addition Doubles</title>

          <p>
            This uses the knowledge of doubles like $2+2=4, 5+5=10$ to introduce multiplication by 2.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Skip Counting</title>

          <p>
            This starts students in understanding multiplication consider skip counting by 2, 3 and 5 such as
            <me>
              \begin{aligned} 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, \ldots \\ 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, \ldots \\ 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 37, \ldots \end{aligned}
            </me>
            and these are precisely multiplying by 2, 3 and 5.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Multiplication Table</title>

          <p>
            Eventually, students need to <em>know</em> the multiplication table within 12.
            This should be introduced slowly, using skip counting and the knowledge of the easier rows.
          </p>
        </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Multiplying by tens or multiples of 10s</title>

      <p>
        A place-value system like our Hindu-Arabic numbers make multiplying by powers of 10 quite easy.
      </p>

      <p>
        The HA number system makes multiplying by 10, 100, 1000, … or multiples of these quite easy.
        <me>
          \begin{aligned}3 \times 10\amp= \text{3 tens}= 30, \\ 42 \times 10\amp= \text{42 tens}= 420, \\ 8 \times 100\amp= \text{8 hundreds}= 800, \\ 192 \times 10\amp= (\text{1 hundred}+ \text{9 tens}+ \text{2 ones}) \times 10 \\\amp= (\text{10 hundreds}+ \text{9 hundreds}+ \text{2 tens}) \\\amp= 1920\end{aligned}
        </me>
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="sect-division">
    <title>Division</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Division of whole numbers is defined in terms of multiplication.
      </p>

      <p>
        The number <m>54 \div 6</m> is defined as the number missing in the multiplication <m>6 \times \boxed{\phantom{X}}= 54</m>.
        <me>
          \underbrace{54}_{\text{dividend}}\div \underbrace{9}_{\text{divisor}} = \underbrace{6}_{\text{quotient}}
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        There are two distinct ways of thinking about division because of the commutative properties of multiplication.
        The division problem <m>18 \div 3</m> can be thought of as either
        <me>
          \begin{aligned} 18\amp= 3 \times \boxed{\phantom{x}}\amp\amp\text{or}\amp18\amp= \boxed{\phantom{x}}\times 3\end{aligned}
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        The answer to either of these is the same, 6, but can be interpreted a bit differently.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The first is “18 is three groups of what size?” (partitive division)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The second is “18 is how many groups of size 3” (measurement division)
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Set Model for partitive and measurement</title>

      <p>
        Division with a set model is interpreted as taking some objects and splitting them into groups.
        The following two diagrams shows <m>18 \div 3</m> with the two interpretations.
        The <em>partitive interpretation</em> means we take the objects and put them in three groups.
        This diagrams shows that:
      </p>

      <image width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.25]
                  \foreach \y in {1,2,3}
                  \foreach \x in {1,2,...,6}  {
                  \begin{scope}[yshift=\y cm, xshift =\x cm] \penguin[scale=0.4] \end{scope}
                  \draw[dashed, rounded corners] (0.6,{\y}) rectangle (6.4,{\y+0.9});
                  }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>

      <p>
        showing three groups of penguins.
        The quotient (which is 6) is the number of penguins in each group.
        Alternatively, the <em>measurement interpretation</em> would be to put the penguins in groups of 3.
        The following diagram shows this:
      </p>

      <image width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.25]
                    \foreach \x in {1,2,...,6}
                    \foreach \y in {1,2,3}
                      {
                    \begin{scope}[yshift=\y cm, xshift =\x cm] \penguin[scale=0.4] \end{scope}
                    \draw[dashed, rounded corners] ({\x-0.4},1) rectangle ({\x+0.4},4);
                    }
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Division Interpretation and Word Problems</title>

      <p>
        Word problems clearly have a division interpretation with them.
        The following example shows two problems with <em>partitive</em> interpretation
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Eight juice boxes with a total of 48 ounces come in a multi-pack.
                  How many ounces is each box? Ans: <m>48 \div 8 = 6</m>
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  A string of length 21 inches is divided into 3 equal pieces.
                  How long is each piece? Ans: <m>21 \div 3=7</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        The follow example shows two word problems with <em>measurement</em> interpretation.
      </p>

      <example>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  One case holds ten juice boxes.
                  How many cases hold 120 juice boxes.
                  Ans: <m>120 \div 10 = 12</m>
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  A case of juice boxes have a total of 96 ounces of juice.
                  If each boxes contains 6 ounces of juice, how many boxes are there? Ans: <m>96 \div 6 = 16</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Division with Remainders</title>

      <p>
        Consider the problem of the number of dozens of eggs in 30 eggs.
        There are an even number of dozens of 30 eggs.
        Two dozen is 24 eggs and three dozen is 36 eggs, so 30 eggs is two dozen plus some left over.
        <me>
          30 = (12 \times \underbrace{\boxed{\phantom{x}}}_{\text{quotient}}) + \underbrace{\boxed{\phantom{x}}}_{\text{remainder}}
        </me>
        and in this case the quotient is 2 and the remainder is 6.
      </p>

      <p>
        Models with division and remainders are quite helpful.
        Below, we show 3 eggs and 2 groups of 12 (dashed) and the remainder of 6.
      </p>

      <image width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[ 
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \foreach \y in {1,2} {
                    \foreach \x in {0,...,11} \draw (\x,\y) pic[scale=0.15] {egg}; 
                    \draw[dashed, rounded corners] (-0.5,{\y-0.4}) rectangle (11.5,{\y+0.5}); 
                    }
                  \foreach \x in {0,...,5} \draw (\x,0) pic[scale=0.15] {egg}; 
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>

      <theorem>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Quotient-Remainder Theorem For any two whole numbers <m>A</m> and <m>k</m> with <m>k\neq 0</m>, there are unique whole numbers <m>q</m> (the quotient) and <m>r</m> (the remainder) such that
            <me>
              A = (k \times q) + r
            </me>
            and <m>0 \leq r \lt k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        This can be demonstrated in a general way with the following rectangular model.
      </p>

      <image width="80%">
        <shortdescription>
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[ 
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.25]
            \draw (0,0) rectangle (5,3); \draw (5,1.25) rectangle (5.5,3); 
            \draw (0.5,0) -- (0.5,3); \draw(4.5,0) -- (4.5,3); 
            \draw (0,0.5) -- (5,0.5); \draw (0,2.5) -- (5.5,2.5); 
            \draw (1,0) -- (1,0.5); \draw (4,0) -- (4,0.5); 
            \draw (1,2.5) -- (1,3); \draw (4,2.5) -- (4,3); 
            \draw (5,1.75) -- (5.5,1.75); \draw (5.25,2.25) node {$\vdots$}; 
            \draw (2.5,0.25) node {$\cdots$}; \draw (2.5,2.75) node {$\cdots$}; 
            \draw (0.25,1.5) node {$\vdots$};
            \draw[brace] (-0.1,0) -- (-0.1,3) node[bracelabel=left] {$k$}; 
            \draw[brace] (5,-0.1) -- (0,-0.1) node[bracelabel=below] {$q$};
            \draw[brace] (5.6,3) -- (5.6,1.25) node[bracelabel=right] {$r$};
            \draw (2.5,1.5) node {$A$ total squares}; 
            \end{tikzpicture}
          ]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>

      <p>
        where the <m>\cdots</m> throughout the diagram indicates that there are an indeterminate number of boxes.
        Note that the number of rows is the divisor and that the remainder <m>r</m> is the number of boxes that don’t fit into a complete row.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is a visual way to see the quotient-remainder theorem, especially the statements that <m>0 \leq r \lt k</m>, that is the number of extra boxes (the remainder) can be 0 and up to (but not including) the number of rows.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection>
      <title>Division by Zero</title>

      <p>
        What is <m>6 \div 0</m>? Using the definition of division, it is the missing factor.
        That is, we can write this as
        <me>
          6= 0 \times \boxed{\phantom{x}}.
        </me>
      </p>

      <p>
        Since nothing can go in the box to make this equation work, the answer to this is <alert>undefined</alert>.
      </p>

      <p>
        We can also think of this in the division interpretation.
        From a <em>partitive</em> interpretation, <m>6 \div 0</m> represents the size of the group if 6 is split into 0 groups.
        Alternatively, from a <em>measurement</em> interpretation, the result is the number of groups of size 0.
        In either case, these don’t make sense.
      </p>

      <p>
        What about <m>0 \div 0</m>? Write this as
        <me>
          0 = 0 \times \boxed{\phantom{x}}
        </me>
        and now in this case, any number works, but we still say this is <alert>undefined</alert>.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>